PUBLIC RESPONSE: {B}

The core issue is that standard poly(dT) capture on BD Rhapsody beads pulls from the 3′ end of the TCR mRNA. CDR3 lies near the 5′ variable-region end, so the 75 × 225 read configuration never reaches it.  

Option B solves this by appending a template-switch oligo (TSO) immediately after the UMI on the bead oligo. During reverse transcription the TSO is incorporated at the 5′ cDNA end, enabling template-switching PCR that selectively amplifies the V(D)J region (including CDR3) while preserving the existing cell-label/UMI architecture and the 75 × 225 sequencing format. All other choices either (a) still rely on 3′-biased capture, (b) require bead redesign or new sequencing modes the user explicitly wants to avoid, or (c) attempt post-capture PCR on material that was never captured in the first place. Thus B is the only modification that fits every stated constraint.}

